 
-------------------------------------*--------------------------------------
                     ShadoeQWK v1.10 / ShadoeCRON v1.0
                            User Documentation
----------------------------------------------------------------------------

I. Overview:

   ShadoeQWK is a utility for the Shadoegate BBS System which enables
 the importing and exporting of popular .QWK mail packets .
 
   All configuration is done through a config file, and ShadoeQWK may
 be set up to operate automatically at set times/events, through
 ShadoeCRON.
 
   ShadoeQWK currently imports messages at a rate of 8 msgs/sec on an
 A3000/25 using normal sized mail traffic. For .QWK packets containing
 USENET traffic, it imports at a rate of 5 msgs/sec. Exporting is
 LIGHTNING fast, too much so for me to accurately measure (if anyone
 out there cares to set up a simple test, I would love to know!).

   ShadoeCRON is just that - a CRON-like utility who's sole purpose in
 life is to sit quietly in the background and wait for certain times of
 day, at which time it notifies the BBS via ENV: variables, and the BBS
 then will perform such actions as dictated by the variable.

   ShadoeQWK and ShadoeCRON were designed to work in tandem as an automatic
 mail pickup/delivery system, but since their inception, I have found a 
 host of other things they are useful for (mainly ShadoeCRON).

II. Installation

   ShadoeQWK and ShadoeCRON should reside somewhere on your logical path.
 Shadoegate: is the usual place, but c: should work as well.
   
   Both utilities require a configuration file, 'message.areas' and 'qwk.cfg' for
 ShadoeQWK, and 'event.dat' for ShadoeCRON. 'message.areas' is also
 the config file for the BBS's message bases, so little or no modification
 may be necessary for ShadoeQWK installation.
  
   'Event.dat' is quite simple to create. Each entry consists of 3 lines
 of text which tells ShadoeCRON what to do for this event. The first line
 of the file contains the total number of events that you will have.
 Each three lines following that represents 1 event of the total,with
 each line having a special meaning.
 
   The first line of an event entry is the EVENT TYPE. ShadoeCRON currently
 understands 3 types of events: MIDNIGHT (reset bbs pointers,etc), 
 MAILRUN (pickup/deliver mail), and DOS (execute a dos command/script).
 
   The second line of the each event entry is the EVENT TIME. Entries are in
 24-hour format, and only the hour and minute are used, followed by a trailing
 colon (:).
 
   The third and last line of each event entry is the EVENT COMMAND. This will
 tell the BBS what command/action to be taken. Any valid DOS command may be
 used.
 
   Example 'event.dat' file:
   -------------------------

4                 --> 4 total events in the file              
MIDNIGHT          --> the first event is of type MIDNIGHT
00:00:            --> ShadoeCRON should perform this event at midnight
flush             --> the command to be executed at this time
MAILRUN           --> the second event is of type MAILRUN
02:00:            --> ShadoeCRON executed this event at 2 am each day.
getmail.bat       --> the command to be executed (script file that gets my mail)
DOS               --> the third event is of type DOS
02:30:            --> ShadoeCRON executes this event at 2:30 am each day.
ShadoeQWK import  --> Command that the BBS will launch at this time.
MAILRUN           --> and so on....
03:00
sendmail.bat

   Now, lets take a look at what we have here:
First, the BBS resets all message pointers,calls,ul/dl,etc.

Second, at 2:00 am The BBS will execute a script which I have setup that
calls my local mail feed and downloads my mail.

Third, the BBS calls ShadoeQWK to import my newly aquired mail.

Lastly, the BBS calls up another script at 3:00 am which will deliver
(export) any new messages found by ShadoeQWK during its import.

   As you can see, this combination can provide some pretty impressive
 power.


III. Setting up message areas for .QWK import/export
----------------------------------------------------

  Setting up your message areas for .QWK compatability is a little tricky,
but once set up, it usually will not need to be changed.
 
  ShadoeQWK requires that a logical device mail: be assigned,
and message bundles are looked for there.

EX: assign mail: dh0:work  (then d/l mail packets to dh0:work)

  For each area that you wish to be .qwk compatable, you must enter
a message area type of 'QWK' (case sensitive). ShadoeQWK will only
perform operations on these areas! (See shadoegate.doc for specifics)

  Each area that is set up for QWK mode must have the directory where the
messages are stored named with the same name of the conference number that
your host BBS (the one you call to get mail) has for that area. Example:
I am calling FooBar BBS and I wish to get the following conferences:

Conference #4944: rec.arts.sf.tv.babylon5
Conference #4158: comp.sys.amiga.marketplace
Conference #4156: comp.sys.amiga.hardware
Conference #4161: comp.sys.amiga.programmer

For these conferences, my shadoegate: directory would look like this:

 SHADOEGATE:
            file/
            doors/
            message/
                   4944/          --> directory for conference #4944
                   4158/          --> directory for conference #4158
                   4156/          --> directory for conference #4156
                   4161/          --> directory for conference #4161

  For each area that is set up as QWK, you also need to enter a netname
for the area. This is the name that will be displayed to the users when
listing areas, etc. Each message area in QWK mode must also be named
the same as the conference number of the BBS you are getting your mail
from. This sounds VERY confusing, but it was done to avoid dependance on
other software's *.NDX and control.dat files, normally found in most
QWK packets. An example entry in message.areas for the above areas would
look like this:
 
4                          --> total number of message areas
4944                       --> the name of this area (same as conference)
1                          --> access level for this area
shadoegate:message/4944/   --> FULL path to this area (dir name same as conf)
20                         --> High message for this area
1                          --> low message for this area
150                        --> number of msgs to keeep in this area
15                         --> last message that was exported
QWK                        --> all-important area-type
rec.arts.sf.tv.babylon5    --> the net-name for this area.
4158                       --> the name of this area (same as conference)
1                          --> access level for this area
shadoegate:message/4158/   --> FULL path to this area (dir name same as conf)
20                         --> High message for this area
1                          --> low message for this area
150                        --> number of msgs to keeep in this area
15                         --> last message that was exported
QWK                        --> all-important area-type
comp.sys.amiga.marketplace --> the net-name for this area.
4156                       --> the name of this area (same as conference)
1                          --> access level for this area
shadoegate:message/4156/   --> FULL path to this area (dir name same as conf)
20                         --> High message for this area
1                          --> low message for this area
150                        --> number of msgs to keeep in this area
15                         --> last message that was exported
QWK                        --> all-important area-type
comp.sys.amiga.hardware    --> the net-name for this area.
4161                       --> the name of this area (same as conference)
1                          --> access level for this area
shadoegate:message/4161/   --> FULL path to this area (dir name same as conf)
20                         --> High message for this area
1                          --> low message for this area
150                        --> number of msgs to keeep in this area
15                         --> last message that was exported
QWK                        --> all-important area-type
comp.sys.amiga.programmer  --> the net-name for this area.

Here is the directory tree again, with additional notes:

    shadoegate:
               file/
               doors/
               message/
                      4944/  this is conf #4944 from my host bbs, so this
                             is really 'rec.arts.sf.tv.babylon5'
                      4158/  this is conf #4158, so this is really
                             comp.sys.amiga.marketplace
                      4156/  this is conf #4156, so this is really
                             comp.sys.amiga.hardware
                      4161/  this is conf #4161, so this is really
                             comp.sys.amiga.programmer

  This system was designed to work directly with PC Board (tm), but
will also work with any other QWK bbs. For those that do not have
the conferencing system, just name your area and your directory whatever
that BBS calls their particular area.

  The config file for ShadoeQWK is called 'qwk.cfg'. It consists of a few
lines of text and is very simple to set up.


  Thats it!.....good luck, and EMAIL me with any questions/comments,etc...
my EMAIL address: rlw@the-matrix.com

LINE 1: command for .lha QWK packets
LINE 2: command for .zip packets
LINE 3: mode command (perently only 'quiet' supported) 
LINE 4: name of the QWK packet to open , minus the .qwk extension
LINE 5: name of .REP packets to be uploaded from users (minus the .rep extension!)

Thats it....simple,right?!

  PS: all message areas MUST HAVE a file called 'lastread' in their
directory! When setting up a new area, just copy the file from any other
area into the new one. This file holds lastread pointers for the area,
and the BBS will recognize faulty pointers and fix them automatically.

                                             -Rick
